Hal,

Here is the access log summary you asked for covering the night of
October 15-16.

At 11:42 PM on October 15, the Gibson's authentication system recorded
a successful login under credentials assigned to Margo Wallace. The
originating terminal was workstation EMC-W-047, located in the third
floor operations wing.

Between 11:44 PM and 11:58 PM, three file write operations were logged
to the financial batch processing directory. The filenames written were
dvinci.exe, dvinci.cfg, and a modification to nightly.sh. All three
writes are associated with the Wallace credential session.

At 12:29 AM, the session was closed from the same terminal. Total
session duration: 47 minutes. No anomalies were flagged by automated
monitoring at the time.

The Gibson executed its nightly batch sequence at 2:00 AM on October 16
as scheduled. The dvinci.exe file ran as part of this sequence. This is
the execution event that Sarah's team later identified as the point of
financial data compromise.

I would note that whoever designed this knew our batch schedule. The
2:00 AM window is not documented outside of internal IT configuration
files. Based on my review of access permissions, Eugene Belford was one
of four individuals who had read access to the cron configuration.

Hal, I want to be clear that I am not drawing conclusions here -- that
is for investigators to do. You should not repeat this last observation
to anyone without speaking to counsel first.

Let me know if you need the full raw logs. I can have them ready within
the hour.

